\subsection{Multi-Provider Stacks and Within-Family Swaps}
\textbf{Multi-provider execution and precision trade-off (Study S2).} Study S2 audits 18 endpoints covering six open-weight checkpoints across three pinned providers each (12 cloud backends: DeepInfra, Parasail, Cloudflare, Groq LPU, SiliconFlow, AtlasCloud, Mistral, Venice, DekaLLM, Crusoe, Fireworks, and CoreWeave; 2,160 calls, zero fallback; Supplement~J). Across S2 providers (20 cells, 6v6), $\Dhyb{}$ reaches 5.93\% EER (17/18 group matches) vs.\ 16.67\% (16/18) for $\Dereg{}$ and 16.67\% for Color MMD-U (3v3: 19.78\%; at $p\le 0.05$, MMD-U rejects 41.67\% genuine and 100.00\% impostor pairs). Stripping API billing-envelope metadata (\texttt{usage.reasoning\_tokens} and \texttt{explicit\_reasoning}) leaves S2 $\Dhyb{}$ unchanged at 5.93\% EER (0.9864 AUC; 5.56\% under pure content syntax), and a color-only composite omitting $\Dside{}$ entirely still reaches 9.63\% EER (17/18 Top-1; Supplement~B). Conversely, RGB distributions remain largely invariant across \texttt{bf16/fp8} and \texttt{int4/fp4} quantizations of the same checkpoint ($\Dereg{}$ AUC/EER: 0.5250/50.00\%; $\Dhyb{}$: 0.8250/30.00\%). Preserving checkpoint identity across heterogeneous serving stacks naturally trades off bit-width sensitivity, making $\Dhyb{}$ suited to cross-host weight matching while leaving bit-width verification out of scope.

\textbf{Within-family alternatives.} Because same-vendor tier substitutions are harder than cross-vendor swaps, Table~7 isolates negative pairs sharing a declared model family. On shallow 3v3 sampling in A ($8\times 6$, 604 same-family negatives), restricting to same-family negatives raises $\Dereg{}$ EER from 5.36\% to 10.10\% (10.45\% same-vendor) and $\Dhyb{}$ from 7.14\% to 12.42\%. At $20\times 6$ (B) or $10\times 30$ (S3), $\Dereg{}$ holds 0.00\% within-family EER, and protocol-matched $\mathrm{B}\rightarrow\mathrm{E}$ transfer maintains 0.00\% within-family FAR.

