\section{Service Monitoring and Controlled Interventions}
\subsection{Response Reuse and Private Pool Subsampling}
Zero-entropy screening eliminates constant-centroid replay (0.00\% acceptance). Because samples drawn from the true reference law on leaked fixed prompts follow the enrolled distribution (Supplement~L), contracts keep prompt pools private ($<10{,}000$ tokens/model for a 64-call C template) against static lookup tables.

To reduce repeated-prompt signatures without incurring zero-shot paraphrase drift, monitors pre-enroll a multi-template pool once and sample a cell-matched subset per audit. Over 200 draws per schedule (Supplement~L), sampling $k=10$ or $16$ of B's 40 enrolled cells cuts per-prompt query frequency by $4\times$ or $2.5\times$ while holding $\Dereg{}$ mean EER at 0.65\% and 0.10\% (3 query reps) or 0.03\% and 0.00\% (7 reps). Pooling $P_0 \cup P_1 \cup P_2$ into a 24-cell private reference ($<11{,}100$ enrollment tokens/model at 4 reps) yields 1.08\% and 0.17\% mean EER at $k=8$ or $12$ cells.

In a controlled numerical continuity stress test on B ($40\times 15$, frozen B8v7 cutoffs; Supplement~L Table~S11), all seven raw-score coordinate perturbations retain 0.00\% re-swept EER and 0.00\% frozen FAR. Raw $\Dereg{}$ absorbs $\pm 1$ and $\pm 8$ random jitter at 0.00\% frozen FRR (11.00\% centered at $\pm 8$), whereas uncalibrated pipeline shifts ($\pm 15$ jitter, global $\pm 8$ shifts, and step-32 channel quantization) cross clean cutoffs as expected (56.00\%--100.00\% flagged).

